What is SearchNode?
Think of it as one possible future of the game.

Suppose your snake can move left, straight or right. Each choice creates a different future state. Each future is represented by a SearchNode.

For example:
Node A :future state after left - score 82
Node B :future state after straight - score 64

Imagine the search is allowed to explore in deeper depths of the future states:
Left → Straight → Right → Left → Straight

After five turns, it decides this is the best sequence. But you only need to make one move now.
`firstMove` remembers that the entire sequence began with Left.

Every child node inherits its parent's firstMove. Only the initial nodes assign it.

How the classes work together
- GameState = what the game looks like at a particular moment.
- SearchNode = one possible future game state, plus information about how you got there.
- BeamSearch = generates and evaluates these nodes, keeping the best candidates. (I think this is later on)

## Phase 2 — implemented

`GameState.simulateTurn(TurnInputs)` resolves all snakes from the same
pre-turn state. `TurnInputs` carries the absolute direction for each live
snake, plus injected next-food and respawn cells when those random outcomes
are needed. The protocol does not reveal the engine RNG state, so those
outcomes cannot be reproduced from an observation alone.

`TurnResult` exposes the resulting state, each attempted destination, food
consumption, deaths, and kill attribution. The simulator handles wrapped
movement, growth as repeated tail cells, tail-vacating collision checks,
body collisions, head-to-head length resolution, and the league respawn
countdown.

The active route planner does not enumerate joint opponent moves or run the
full simulator. For each of our legal first moves, it projects the board one
turn ahead by clearing tails that will actually vacate, using `pendingGrowth`
and whether the snake could eat the current apple. A bounded A* search then
tracks our full body over time, including tail movement and food growth, and
rejects self-colliding routes. Among shortest routes it prefers more open
space after eating; it may accept a route up to two moves longer if that
substantially increases reachable space. A separate immediate check rejects a
first step that an equal-or-longer opponent can reach with one of its legal
straight/left/right moves, accounting for food growth for both snakes. If
routes tie on food distance and our reachable area, a forced immediate kill
against a shorter opponent, then reduced immediate escape moves for shorter
opponents, are tie-breakers.

The full simulator remains available as a foundation for later prediction.
It is not currently on the active decision path; exhaustive scenario
simulation was too conservative for the current route-planning goal. The
route's future board treats opponents' bodies as static after their possible
first-turn tail movement. The A* node and expansion budgets bound work under
the 50 ms deadline; when no food route is found within the budget, the planner
falls back to the legal move with most reachable space.
